      "writtenOn": "2025-07-09T09:42:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Compared to the previous version, I found the blue icons more pleasant than the current orange ones, as most of the requests were in state building when I viewed the page. I think it makes sense to use a different color for an \"active\" state like `building` compared to other states like `requested` or `hold`.\n\nHowever, comparing this page to the status page - where we use orange/warning mainly for error-like states (merge_conflict on a queue item and post_failure, node_failure, retry_limit on jobs) - viewing this page with a lot of building nodes gets a negative touch, like something is really wrong here, although the nodes are just building. I think this doesn\u0027t really fit to the design language of Zuul where we usually assume that everything succeeds until it proves different and therefore most of the squares are green on the status page.\n\nIf we would take over this design language, we would make the `building` icons also green, but that would make them hard to differentiate from `ready` nodes (which I think is more important for nodes than it is for queue items on the status page). This leads me again to the idea that we might want to give `building` nodes a little animation. Then we would have a transition from `building` (green with animation) to `ready` (green without animation) or red in case something went wrong.",
      "revId": "17fb8d2c794f91779dad703a2b1e79d088c1808d",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f397c2e9_9706e8ab",
        "filename": "web/src/Misc.jsx",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 1
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-09T13:52:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason for the change is to have consistent colors for the states on both the nodes page and the nodeset requests page.  I agree with you that the orange color for building nodes is off-putting, but the real driver here is that on the *nodes* page, we want all of the in-use nodes to be blue.  That *does* match the status page (because those jobs are (generally) going to be blue).\n\nSo with:\n\n1) in-use \u003d blue\n2) used/failed/outdated \u003d red (these are all nodes that should be deleted)\n\nWe\u0027re running out of colors for building.  It\u0027s not ideal, but at least \"warning\" (orange) suggests some kind of activity.\n\nWe could consider having it pulse, but if we want to do the same thing on the nodes list page, we would have the word \"building\" be pulsing blue.  That might be annoying and difficult to read (it\u0027s already not easy to read blue words).\n\nPerhaps we should just introduce a new color; I\u0027m open to suggestions.",
      "parentUuid": "1e05a59f_b2305975",
      "revId": "17fb8d2c794f91779dad703a2b1e79d088c1808d",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d15747b7_8f5166ec",
        "filename": "web/src/Misc.jsx",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 27952
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-10T09:02:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think using blue for in-use nodes makes sense and would fit to the colors for the job progress bars on the status page.\n\nI also had the impression that we\u0027re running out of colors for that purpose. I think it\u0027s fine to continue with this and improve the colors later on (we did the same with the status page). It looks like we are on the same track here.\n\nRegarding the pulse animation: If we want to use the same colors also for the state words on the node-list page that might indeed be a little problematic. I still think that an animation would improve the overall look-and-feel of the noderequest page. But we can still experiment with this later on.",
      "parentUuid": "f397c2e9_9706e8ab",
      "revId": "17fb8d2c794f91779dad703a2b1e79d088c1808d",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
    },
